<Article slug="choosing-a-mug">
  <p>
    Most people have one mug they always reach for. A handmade mug can be that one for years, and
    a few simple checks will help you find it, in person or online.
  </p>

  <h2>Weight</h2>
  <p>
    Stoneware has some heft, and that is part of its appeal: it holds heat and feels solid in the
    hand. But a good mug should still feel balanced when it is full. If you are buying online, the
    height and width on the listing tell you a lot. Compare them with a mug you already like.
  </p>

  <h2>The handle</h2>
  <p>
    Think about how you hold a mug. Some people hook one finger through, others want room for two
    or three, and some wrap their whole hand around the body and ignore the handle completely. A
    handle should leave space between your knuckles and the hot wall. If you prefer to cradle your
    coffee, a handleless <a href="/learn/tea-vessels/">yunomi style cup</a> may suit you better.
  </p>

  <h2>The lip</h2>
  <p>
    The rim is the part you actually touch with your mouth, so it matters more than it looks. A
    slightly rounded, smooth lip is the most comfortable to drink from. In a soda kiln the rim
    often catches extra flashing, which is one of the nicest places to see the fire's colour.
  </p>

  <h2>Volume</h2>
  <p>
    A standard coffee pour is about 8 to 12 ounces. If you take milk, or like a big morning
    coffee, look for something larger. For espresso or cappuccino, smaller and wider is better, so
    there is room for foam.
  </p>

  <h2>Inside colour</h2>
  <p>
    A pale glaze inside, like <a href="/collection/?surface=bone">Bone</a>, shows you the colour of
    your coffee or tea. A dark one, like <a href="/collection/?surface=char">Char</a>, hides stains
    and looks dramatic against the outside. Neither is better. It is just personal.
  </p>

  <h2>Care</h2>
  <p>
    Every mug I make is stoneware fired to about cone 10, and is food, microwave and dishwasher
    safe. Handwashing will keep the surface looking its best for longest. The
    <a href="/care/">care page</a> has the details, and the
    <a href="/collection/?category=mugs">mugs</a> are all one of a kind.
  </p>
</Article>
